            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Portuguese for the past, then the Portuguese for to happen."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: o futuro",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "o futuro — \"the future\". Portuguese has two futures. The everyday one is ir with an infinitive: Vou falar. — \"I am going to speak.\" The one-word future adds endings to the infinitive: falarei, falarás, falará. It is the one you will read."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "futuro from Latin futurus, \"about to be\" (EtymDB 2.1), a form of esse, \"to be\" — a learned borrowing from Latin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for this chapter."
            }
          ]
        },
